.\" create.3 - man page for the create() function (libcreate)
.\"
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH CREATE 3 "2026-10-23" "libcreate 1.0.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
create \- create or truncate a file (the correctly spelled creat)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <create.h>
.PP
.BI "int create(const char *" path ", mode_t " mode );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B create()
creates a new file or truncates an existing one.
It is equivalent to calling:
.PP
.in +4n
.nf
open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, mode)
.fi
.in
.PP
This provides identical behavior to the POSIX
.BR creat (2)
function, but with the correct spelling.
.PP
If the file named by
.I path
does not exist, it is created with permissions
.IR mode " & ~" umask .
.PP
If the file already exists, it is truncated to zero length.
Its existing permissions are
.B not
changed by
.IR mode .
.PP
The returned file descriptor is opened for writing only.
Attempting to read from it will fail with
.BR EBADF .
.PP
Passing
.B NULL
as
.I path
is undefined behavior, as with
.BR creat (2).
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, a non-negative file descriptor is returned.
.PP
On failure, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
Permission denied (search permission denied on a path component, or
write permission denied on the parent directory).
.TP
.B EISDIR
.I path
refers to an existing directory.
.TP
.B EMFILE
The per-process limit on open file descriptors has been reached.
.TP
.B ENAMETOOLONG
.I path
or a component of
.I path
is too long.
.TP
.B ENFILE
The system-wide limit on open files has been reached.
.TP
.B ENOENT
A directory component of
.I path
does not exist, or
.I path
is an empty string.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
No space left on the device containing the file.
.TP
.B ENOTDIR
A component of the path prefix of
.I path
is not a directory.
.SH NOTES
The name
.B create
comes from Ken Thompson's famous remark about the design of UNIX:
.PP
.RS
.I "What's one of the things I'd do differently if I were redesigning the UNIX system? I'd spell creat with an \[oq]e\[cq]."
.RE
.PP
This library exists to finally give that missing \[oq]e\[cq] a home.
.PP
.B create()
is thread-safe and async-signal-safe to the same degree as
.BR open (2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (2),
.BR creat (2),
.BR close (2),
.BR umask (2)
.SH HISTORY
.B libcreate
1.0.0, released 2026.
